\]

Since the integrand is odd, the integral over symmetric limits (from \(-\infty\) to \(\infty\)) is zero. 

To confirm this using contour integration, consider the complex integral \( I = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \frac{x e^{ix}}{x^2 + 4} \, dx \). Using contour integration, we close the contour in the upper half-plane due to the exponential decay of \( e^{iz} \) there. The integrand has a pole at \( z = 2i \).

The residue at \( z = 2i \) is calculated as follows:

\[
\text{Residue at } z = 2i = \lim_{z \to 2i} \left( (z - 2i) \frac{z e^{iz}}{(z - 2i)(z + 2i)} \right) = \frac{2i e^{-2}}{4i} = \frac{1}{2} e^{-2}
\]

Applying the residue theorem, the integral \( I \) is:

\[
I = 2\pi i \cdot \frac{1}{2} e^{-2} = \pi i e^{-2}
\]

Taking the real part of \( I \) (since the original integral is the real part of \( I \)):

\[
\text{Re}(I) = \text{Re}(\pi i e^{-2}) = 0
\]

Thus, the integral is confirmed to be zero. 

