To determine whether \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right) \) can be expressed as a finite tower of nested real radicals, we consider the constructibility of regular polygons. A regular \( n \)-gon is constructible if and only if \( n \) is a product of a power of 2 and distinct Fermat primes. Fermat primes are primes of the form \( 2^{2^k} + 1 \).

- \( 17 \) is a Fermat prime (\( 2^4 + 1 \)), which Gauss showed allows a constructible 17-gon, so \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{17}\right) \) can be expressed with nested radicals.
- \( 257 \) is also a Fermat prime (\( 2^8 + 1 \)), which makes the 257-gon constructible. Therefore, the cosine term \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right) \) can also be expressed using a finite tower of real radicals.

The key reason is that the Galois group of the cyclotomic field extension for a Fermat prime is cyclic, allowing successive quadratic (i.e., square root) field extensions. Since \( 257 \) is a Fermat prime, its cyclotomic field's extension degree is a power of 2 (\( 256 \)), ensuring the minimal polynomial can be solved with nested square roots. Thus, \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right) \) can indeed be written as a finite tower of nested real radicals.

\boxed{Yes}<|im_end|>
