To determine whether \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right) \) can be expressed as a finite tower of nested real radicals, we start by noting that 257 is a Fermat prime, specifically \( 2^8 + 1 \). A regular \( n \)-gon is constructible with a compass and straightedge if and only if \( n \) is a product of distinct Fermat primes and a power of 2. Since 257 is a Fermat prime and constructible, its cosine values can be expressed using radicals.

The minimal polynomial of \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right) \) over the rationals is related to the 257th cyclotomic polynomial. The degree of this polynomial is half the degree of the cyclotomic polynomial for \( n = 257 \), which is \( \frac{\phi(257)}{2} = \frac{256}{2} = 128 \). Since 128 is a power of 2, the field extension \( \mathbb{Q}(\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right)) \) over \( \mathbb{Q} \) is a tower of quadratic extensions. Each quadratic extension can be represented using square roots, hence real radicals.

Therefore, \( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{257}\right) \) can indeed be expressed as a finite tower of nested real radicals.

