To determine if the induced map \(\varphi^*: A(Y) \to A(X)\) is injective when \(\varphi: X \to Y\) is a dominant morphism between affine varieties, we start by recalling key concepts. A dominant morphism has a dense image in \(Y\). For affine varieties, which are irreducible, a regular function vanishing on a dense subset must vanish everywhere, since their zero sets are closed and dense subsets close to entire sets are closed.

Consider a non-zero element \(f \in A(Y)\). Since \(Y\) is irreducible, the support of \(f\) (where \(f \neq 0\)) is dense. If \(\varphi\) is dominant, its image is dense in \(Y\). Thus, \(f\) vanishes on the image of \(\varphi\) only if it vanishes everywhere on \(Y\). Therefore, the kernel of \(\varphi^*\) must be trivial because any non-zero \(f\) evaluated on the image cannot be zero. Hence, \(\varphi^*\) is injective.

This reasoning is confirmed by the fundamental theorem in algebraic geometry that a regular function vanishing on a dense subset must be identically zero for irreducible varieties. Therefore, the induced map \(\varphi^*\) is injective.

