To evaluate the infinite product \(\prod_{n=2}^\infty \dfrac{n^3 - 1}{n^3 + 1}\), we start by factoring the numerator and denominator using the difference and sum of cubes formulas:

\[
n^3 - 1 = (n - 1)(n^2 + n + 1)
\]
\[
n^3 + 1 = (n + 1)(n^2 - n + 1)
\]

Thus, each term in the product can be written as:

\[
\dfrac{(n - 1)(n^2 + n + 1)}{(n + 1)(n^2 - n + 1)}
\]

We can separate this into two parts:

\[
\dfrac{n - 1}{n + 1} \quad \text{and} \quad \dfrac{n^2 + n + 1}{n^2 - n + 1}
\]

We analyze each part separately. For the linear terms \(\prod_{n=2}^N \dfrac{n - 1}{n + 1}\):

\[
\prod_{n=2}^N \dfrac{n - 1}{n + 1} = \dfrac{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdots (N - 1)}{3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5 \cdots (N + 1)} = \dfrac{(N - 1)!}{(N + 1)! / (1 \cdot 2)} = \dfrac{2}{N(N + 1)}
\]

For the quadratic terms \(\prod_{n=2}^N \dfrac{n^2 + n + 1}{n^2 - n + 1}\):

Notice that the denominator of the quadratic term for \(n\) is the numerator of the next term for \(n+1\). This results in telescoping:

\[
\prod_{n=2}^N \dfrac{n^2 + n + 1}{n^2 - n + 1} = \dfrac{(2^2 + 2 + 1)(3^2 + 3 + 1) \cdots (N^2 + N + 1)}{3 \cdot 7 \cdot 13 \cdots (N^2 - N + 1)}
\]

This simplifies to:

\[
\dfrac{N^2 + N + 1}{3}
\]

Combining both parts, the product up to \(N\) is:

\[
\dfrac{2}{N(N + 1)} \cdot \dfrac{N^2 + N + 1}{3} = \dfrac{2(N^2 + N + 1)}{3N(N + 1)}
\]

Taking the limit as \(N \to \infty\):

\[
\lim_{N \to \infty} \dfrac{2(N^2 + N + 1)}{3N(N + 1)} = \lim_{N \to \infty} \dfrac{2N^2(1 + 1/N + 1/N^2)}{3N^2(1 + 1/N)} = \dfrac{2}{3}
\]

Thus, the value of the infinite product is:

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{2}{3}}
\]<|im_end|>
