Given a profinite group \( G \), we consider the Grothendieck groups \( K(G, \mathbb{Z}_\ell) \) and \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \) for the derived categories of finitely generated \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-modules and \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \)-modules with continuous \( G \)-actions, respectively. The reduction map induces a group homomorphism \( K(G, \mathbb{Z}_\ell) \to K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \) via \( [T] \mapsto [T \otimes^L_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} \mathbb{F}_\ell] \). We need to determine if \( [T \otimes^L_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} \mathbb{F}_\ell] = 0 \) in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \) for a finitely generated \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-module \( T \) killed by some power of \( \ell \).

1. **Structure of \( T \)**: \( T \) is a finitely generated \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-module killed by some \( \ell^m \), implying it is a finite abelian \( \ell \)-group. \( T \) can be expressed as a direct sum of cyclic \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-modules \( \mathbb{Z}/\ell^{n_i} \mathbb{Z}_\ell \).
2. **Derived Tensor Product**: The derived tensor product \( T \otimes^L_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} \mathbb{F}_\ell \) computes the Tor groups \( \text{Tor}_i^{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}(T, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \).
3. **Tor Computation**: For \( T = \mathbb{Z}/\ell^m \mathbb{Z}_\ell \):
   - A projective resolution of \( T \) over \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \) is \( 0 \to \mathbb{Z}_\ell \xrightarrow{\ell^m} \mathbb{Z}_\ell \to T \to 0 \).
   - Tensoring with \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \), we get \( 0 \to \mathbb{F}_\ell \xrightarrow{0} \mathbb{F}_\ell \to T \otimes \mathbb{F}_\ell \to 0 \).
   - The Tor groups are \( \text{Tor}_0^{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}(T, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \cong \mathbb{F}_\ell \) and \( \text{Tor}_1^{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}(T, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \cong \mathbb{F}_\ell \).
4. **Class in Grothendieck Group**: The derived tensor product results in a complex \( 0 \to \mathbb{F}_\ell \xrightarrow{0} \mathbb{F}_\ell \to 0 \). Its class in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \) is \( [\mathbb{F}_\ell] - [\mathbb{F}_\ell] = 0 \).

**Example Verification**: For \( T = \mathbb{Z}/\ell \mathbb{Z}_\ell \), the class vanishes since both \( \text{Tor}_0 \) and \( \text{Tor}_1 \) are \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \). General \( T \) being a direct sum of such terms preserves this cancellation.

Thus, the class \( [T \otimes^L_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} \mathbb{F}_\ell] \) vanishes in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \).

