Given a profinite group \( G \) and considering the Grothendieck groups \( K(G, \mathbb{Z}_\ell) \) and \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \), the reduction map is defined by sending the class \([T]\) to \([T \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}^L \mathbb{F}_\ell]\). The question asks whether this reduced class is zero in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \) if the \(\mathbb{Z}_\ell\)-module \( T \) with continuous \( G \)-action is killed by some power of \(\ell\).

1. **Key Concepts**:
   - \( K(G, \mathbb{Z}_\ell) \) is the Grothendieck group of the derived category of finitely generated \(\mathbb{Z}_\ell\)-modules with continuous \( G \)-action.
   - The derived catenator product \( T \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}^L \mathbb{F}_\ell \) involves Tor terms.
   - If \( T \) is killed by \(\ell^m\), it is an \(\mathbb{F}_{\ell^{m}}\)-module, hence a direct sum of copies of \(\mathbb{F}_{\ell^{m}}\).

2. **Tensor Product Analysis**:
   - For \( T \) killed by \(\ell^m\), \( T \) is a finite \(\mathbb{Z}_\ell\)-module of \(\mathbb{F}_{\ell^{m}}\).
   - The derived tensor product \( T \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}^L \mathbb{F}_\ell \) incorporates Tor terms. Specifically, using projective resolutions, the zeroth Tor term \( \text{Tor}_0 \) is \( T \otimes \mathbb{F}_\ell \), which is an \(\mathbb{F}_\ell\)-module.
   - The first Tor term \( \text{Tor}_1 \) is calculated as \(\mathbb{F}_\ell\) due to the Tor isomorphism over \(\mathbb{Z}_\ell\).

3. **Grothendieck Group Equivalence**:
   - The class \([T \otimes^L_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} \mathbb{F}_\ell]\) in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \) is the alternating sum of Tor terms: \([ \text{Tor}_0 ] - [ \text{Tor}_1 ]\).
   - For \( T \) killed by \(\ell^m\), this results in \([ \mathbb{F}_\ell ] - [ \mathbb{F}_\ell ] = 0\), meaning the class is zero.

4. **Example and Verification**:
   - Taking \( T = \mathbb{F}_{\ell^m} \), the derived tensor product simplifies to \( [\mathbb{F}_\ell] - [\mathbb{F}_\ell] = 0 \).
   - This holds for any finite direct sum of such modules, ensuring the Grothendieck class is zero.

Thus, the reduction map sends the class of \( T \) to zero in the Grothendieck group \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \).

