a_0 = 1, \; b_0 = 0
\end{cases}
\]
We need to calculate the value of \(\frac{a_{20}}{a_{20} + b_{20}}\).

First, we represent the system in matrix form:
\[
\begin{bmatrix} a_{n+1} \\ b_{n+1} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 & -4 \\ 4 & 6 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} a_n \\ b_n \end{bmatrix}
\]

The matrix \(M = \begin{bmatrix} -2 & -4 \\ 4 & 6 \end{bmatrix}\) has eigenvalues \(\lambda = 2\) (with multiplicity 2) and is defective. The matrix \(N = M - 2I\) (where \(I\) is the identity matrix) is nilpotent with \(N^2 = 0\). Using the binomial theorem for matrices, we find:
\[
M^n = 2^n I + n \cdot 2^{n-1} N
\]

For the initial vector \(v_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}\), we compute \(M^{20} v_0\):
\[
M^{20} = 2^{20} I + 20 \cdot 2^{19} N
\]
\[
N v_0 = \begin{bmatrix} -4 & -4 \\ 4 & 4 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}
\]
\[
M^{20} v_0 = 2^{20} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + 20 \cdot 2^{19} \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2^{20} - 4 \cdot 20 \cdot 2^{19} \\ 4 \cdot 20 \cdot 2^{19} \end{bmatrix}
\]

Simplifying each component:
\[
a_{20} = 2^{20} (1 - 2 \cdot 20) = 2^{20} (1 - 40) = -39 \cdot 2^{20}
\]
\[
b_{20} = 4 \cdot 20 \cdot 2^{19} = 20 \cdot 2 \cdot 2^{20} = 40 \cdot 2^{20}
\]

The sum \(a_{20} + b_{20}\) is:
\[
a_{20} + b_{20} = -39 \cdot 2^{20} + 40 \cdot 2^{20} = 2^{20}
\]

Thus, the ratio is:
\[
\frac{a_{20}}{a_{20} + b_{20}} = \frac{-39 \cdot 2^{20}}{2^{20}} = -39
\]

The final answer is \(\boxed{-39}\).<|im_end|>
