To determine the cardinality of the set of functions from the set of natural numbers \( \mathbb{N} \) to the power set of \( \mathbb{N} \), \( \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \), we start by noting the following key points:

1. The cardinality of \( \mathbb{N} \) is \( \aleph_0 \).
2. The cardinality of \( \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \) is \( 2^{\aleph_0} \), which is the cardinality of the continuum.
3. The cardinality of the set of functions from a set \( A \) to a set \( B \) is given by \( |B|^{|A|} \).

Applying these points:
- The set of functions from \( \mathbb{N} \) to \( \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \) has cardinality \( |\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})|^{|\mathbb{N}|} \).
- Substituting the known cardinalities, this becomes \( (2^{\aleph_0})^{\aleph_0} \).
- Using the rule for cardinal exponentiation \( (a^b)^c = a^{b \cdot c} \), we get \( 2^{\aleph_0 \cdot \aleph_0} \).
- Since \( \aleph_0 \cdot \aleph_0 = \aleph_0 \), this simplifies to \( 2^{\aleph_0} \).

Thus, the cardinality of the set of functions from \( \mathbb{N} \) to \( \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \) is the same as the cardinality of \( \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \), which is \( 2^{\aleph_0} \).

\[
\boxed{2^{\aleph_0}}
\]<|im_end|>
